      {
        "id": "s0slEx02Trvnf6Sz",
        "title": "Collecting your own data",
        "content": {
          "type": "Text",
          "format": "html",
          "text": [
            "<p><strong>CHANGE THIS TO &quot;design your own study&quot; and precede with a class discussion.</strong></p><p><strong>Give them access to rulers, that distance to reaction time table, the energy sticks, and other things.</strong></p><p><strong>They should not have to all do the same study, but at least one group should do a reaction time study [to show that the reaction time is too fast to be accounted for by the chemicals] --&gt; there can be more than one reaction time study, though (and they can be designed differently), and ideally at least one that looks at temperature</strong></p><p><strong></strong></p><p><strong>How fast do your muscles move?</strong></p><p></p><p></p><p>How long does it take you to react to signals, such as a changing street crossing sign or catching a ball? </p><p>As explained in this <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=gJEuT3aKcMI&amp;themeRefresh=1\">video,</a> you will work with a partner to measure your reaction time, comparing your dominant and non-dominant hand. </p><p>Which hand do you expect to react faster: your dominant or non-dominant hand? Explain your prediction in your workspace. </p><p> To find out if your prediction is accurate, follow these steps:</p><p>1. Decide who is the Dropper and who is the Catcher.</p><p>2. The catcher should extend their <strong>dominant</strong> hand (the hand used for writing) with the index finger and thumb about 1 cm apart. The dropper should hold the ruler at the top such that catcher has their thumb and index fingers on either side of the zero cm mark.</p><p>3. When the catcher says they are ready. the dropper should count up, in their head, to a number between 3 and 5 (use a different number each time). When the number is reached, they drop the ruler.</p><p>4. Catcher catches the ruler as fast as they can. Ignore any times the ruler falls on the ground. </p><p>5. Enter the ruler cm value closest to Catcher’s pinch point in the &quot;distance&quot; column in the table below (you should first drag the table to your worksheet). </p><p>6. Repeat steps 2-5 five times.</p><p>7. Repeat the process with the catcher&#x27;s non-dominant hand. </p>"
          ]
        }
      },
